Let $d = \gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1)$.  Then $d$ divides both $n + 7$ and $2n + 1$.  Since $d$ divides $2n + 1$, it also divides $2(n + 7) = 2n + 14$.  Therefore, $d$ divides the difference of $2n + 1$ and $2n + 14$, which is $13$.  Hence, $d$ is either $1$ or $13$.

If $d = 1$, then $\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1) = 1$.  If $d = 13$, then $\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1) = 13$.  Since $13 > 1$, the greatest possible value of $\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1)$ is $\boxed{13}$.

[asy]
import graph;

// Draw the graph of the function f(x) = x + 7
real f(real x) {
  return x + 7;
}

draw(graph(f, -10, 10), red);

// Draw the graph of the function g(x) = 2x + 1
real g(real x) {
  return 2*x + 1;
}

draw(graph(g, -10, 10), blue);

label("$n + 7$", (10, f(10)), NE);
label("$2n + 1$", (10, g(10)), NE);
[/asy]

The greatest possible value of $\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1)$ is $\boxed{13}$.
